WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.511
A card terminal keeps a connection to your server open, and sends one request every ten seconds.

2
00:00:08.511 --> 00:00:11.417
The server gives that connection its own thread.

3
00:00:11.417 --> 00:00:13.536
What does the thread do in between?

4
00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:17.861
One level down, the kernel keeps a receive buffer for each socket.

5
00:00:17.861 --> 00:00:20.777
The thread calls read, and the buffer is empty.

6
00:00:20.777 --> 00:00:25.925
So the kernel takes the thread off the CPU, and parks it on the socket's wait list.

7
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.158
A packet arrives.

8
00:00:27.158 --> 00:00:32.223
The kernel puts its bytes in the buffer and wakes the thread, and read returns them.

9
00:00:32.223 --> 00:00:36.564
The thread replies, calls read again, and parks for another ten seconds.

10
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:40.861
It's a real OS thread, with its own stack of about a megabyte.

11
00:00:40.861 --> 00:00:46.209
And a thread dump calls it RUNNABLE, because the JVM sees it running native code.

12
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:52.453
Now ten thousand terminals connect, and each request takes 150 milliseconds.

13
00:00:52.453 --> 00:00:55.253
How many threads are busy at any moment?

14
00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:00.674
That's a thousand requests a second.

15
00:01:00.674 --> 00:01:04.489
Times 0.15 seconds, Little's law says 150 in flight.

16
00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:10.725
But the server holds ten thousand threads, and almost all of them sit parked in read.

17
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:17.745
In 1999, Dan Kegel called this the C10K problem: ten thousand clients at once.

18
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:23.549
The way out: make the sockets non-blocking, so a read on an empty buffer returns at once.

19
00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.751
Then one thread asks the kernel: which sockets are ready right now?

20
00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:33.242
select and poll take the whole list on every call, which gets slow with thousands.

21
00:01:33.242 --> 00:01:38.647
epoll and kqueue keep the list inside the kernel, and hand back only the ready ones.

22
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:41.659
The thread serves those, then asks again.

23
00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:43.090
That's an event loop.

24
00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.201
Its price: it must never block.

25
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:50.215
Here is the line that parks a thread, in Java and in Kotlin.

26
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.339
So a blocking read holds a whole thread for the whole wait.

27
00:01:56.339 --> 00:02:00.711
Thread-per-connection needs one thread per open socket, busy or idle.

28
00:02:00.711 --> 00:02:03.372
Readiness lets one thread watch thousands.

29
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.392
Quick check.

30
00:02:04.392 --> 00:02:10.790
Five hundred terminals each send one request every five seconds, and each takes 100 milliseconds.

31
00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:15.737
How many threads does the server hold, and how many requests are in flight?
